				"name": "Resize",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"6": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TRANSFORMATION\n\nIn the previous tutorials you have learned how to work with shapes and textures.\nNow we want to slowly build towards making animated visuals with these data types.\n\nIn this tutorial we're taking a look at a couple of nodes that allow you to do the basic transformations that you'll be doing all the time: moving, scaling, rotating, cropping and resizing. \n\nWe will also learn how to construct a Float2 type out of single floats."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 221.5899505615234375,
					"width": 583.39630126953125,
					"x": 649.9228515625,
					"y": -356.181610107421875
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffd0c117",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"7": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TRANSFORM\n\nThe Transform node is the \"expanded\" version of the Move node.\nWith the Transform node comes the ability to rotate and scale the input.\nThe Transform node can also handle other data types like Textures, Slices and Meshes.\n\nWhen working with the Transform node you work in either pixel space or normalized space.\nThis is relevant when using the translation parameter.\nTypes like Mesh2D and Shape2D work in normalized space which scales with the aspect ratio.\nTypes like Texture2D and Slice work in pixel space.\n\nAssignment: Try moving the rectangle out of the screen using the translation parameters. You'll notice that it should move out of the screen around the 1.77 mark. That is no coincidence since this example is done on a 1920x1080 resolution, which is a 16:9 aspect ratio (16\/9 = 1.77).\n\nAssignment: Try moving the Wire logo out of the screen using the translation parameters.\nYou'll notice that you need much larger values to get this done because you are working in pixel space and the resolution is 1920x1080."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 749.848876953125,
					"width": 817.4527587890625,
					"x": 1492.47216796875,
					"y": -99.46385955810546875
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ff02bbff",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"8": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "MOVE\n\nThe move node is a simple and fast way to move your shapes around.\nIt takes a Float2 type for X and Y movement.\n\nIn this example we are using the Float2 node to construct a Float2.\nWe use two Float In nodes, renamed to X and Y, to construct the Float2.\n\nAssignment: Use the X and Y sliders to move the shape around.\n\nAssignment: Delete the Float2 node and connect the outlet of X to the translation inlet of the Move node. The value of X will be used for both the X and Y position, limiting its movement to a diagonal line.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 750.968017578125,
					"width": 817.2315673828125,
					"x": 650.15869140625,
					"y": -100.123992919921875
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffff6a00",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"9": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "CROP & TRIM\n\nCrop and Trim can be used when you need to cut parts off of a texture.\nThe most important difference between the two nodes is that the result of the Crop node will still be at the resolution of the incoming signal. The Trim node will instead reduce resolution.\n\nAdditionally, the Crop node can operate in both normalized and pixel space and can be modulated using any flow type. The Trim node on the other hand only operates in attribute flow and only in pixel space.\n\nAssignment: both crop and trim the test card image so that it only shows the vertical color bar.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 740.57098388671875,
					"width": 817.53118896484375,
